   <exercise>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      We're going to look at the <term>convection equation</term>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the Fourier transform to solve the
                
                convection equation:
                <md>
                    \frac{\partial u}{\partial t}= k\frac{\partial^{2} u}{\partial x^{2}}+ c\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}
                </md>
                for <m>-\infty &lt; x &lt; \infty</m> and <m>u(x,0) = f(x)</m>.
                You will want to use the following:
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The four properties of the Fourier Transform discussed in class
                        </p>
                    </li>
        
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The shift theorem found on one of the worksheets that gives
                            <m>{\fourier[f(x-\beta)] = e^{i\omega\beta}\fourier[f(x)]}</m>.
                            (NOTE: this applies when you have ONE <m>\fourier[...]</m>
                            multiplied by <m>e^{i\omega\beta}</m>, if you have two
                            <m>\fourier[...]\fourier[...]</m> multiplied by
                            <m>e^{i\omega\beta}</m>, you will need to FIRST combine the two
                            Fourier Transform pieces into one using the convolution property,
                            and then apply the shift theorem to EVERYTHING in
                            <m>\fourier[...]</m>.)
                        </p>
                    </li>
        
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Convolution
                            <m>f(x) * g(x) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty}f(u) g(x-u)du</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
        
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Hint 1: follow the notes for how we solved the heat equation using
                            the Fourier Transform and adapt when necessary
                        </p>
                    </li>
        
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Hint 2: the Fourier Transform is really just an integral, how does a
                            sum break up under an integral?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
       Given your solution to part (a) above, find the appropriate influence function
              <m>G(x,t; z, 0)</m>.
              Consider the initial condition
              <md>
                  f(x) = \delta_{0}(x)
              </md>
              which means <m>f(x)=0</m> for all <m>x\ne 0</m> and
              <m>\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\delta_{0}(x)~ dx = 1</m>.
              Sketch the corresponding solution <m>u(x,t)</m> for various values of
              <m>t&gt;0</m>.
              What is the significance of the term <m>c\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}</m> in
              the equation?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the 6 complex numbers <m>z</m> such that <m>z^{6} = -1</m> and show where
                they are located in the complex plane.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  

    
